of work. And that may be true. But there is much more to him than that. Chief engineer Joseph Strauss was maniacally devoted to worker safety. The job of bridgeman was very dangerous. 60 mile an hour gusts of wind could come out of nowhere and sweep men into the water. So it made a rule that all workers had to wear clip-on safety lines. And a lot of the macho tough guys wouldn't wear them and complained. But on the Golden Gate Bridge build, if you didn't wear one, you were fired. And eventually everyone fell in line.
家伙。这或许是真的。但他的远不止于此。首席工程师约瑟夫·斯特劳斯对工人的安全有着近乎狂热的执着。桥梁工人的工作非常危险。时速 60 英里的阵风会毫无征兆地袭来，将工人卷入水中。因此他制定了一条规则：所有工人都必须佩戴夹扣式安全绳。许多大男子主义的硬汉不愿意佩戴并抱怨连天。但在金门大桥的建设过程中，如果你不戴安全绳，你就会被解雇。最终所有人都服从了规定。

45
00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:52.000
He was also one of the first chief engineers to insist on hard hats on the job. But the ultimate expression of his commitment to worker safety was when he spent over $130,000 on a safety net to be installed as work began on the bridge's roadbed. It was the most expensive, elaborate safety device ever conceived for a major construction site. The net ended up saving 19 lives. 19 men fell off the bridge during construction and fell safely into the net. It spanned across the entire length of the bridge and stuck out about 10 feet wider than the roadbed on each side because with those winds, people didn't just fall straight down. They flew some distance and the net caught them all.
他也是最早坚持在工地上佩戴安全帽的首席工程师之一。但他对工人安全的承诺的终极体现，是他在大桥路面开工时花费了 13.0 万多美元安装了一张安全网。这是为大型建筑工地构思的最昂贵、最复杂的安全装置。这张网最终挽救了 19 条生命。有 19 名工人在施工期间从大桥上失足跌落，并安全地落在了网上。它横跨大桥的全长，并且在两侧比路面突出了大约 10 英尺，因为伴随着那些强风，人们不仅仅是笔直往下掉。他们会飞出一段距离，而安全网把他们全都接住了。

46
00:25:52.000 --> 00:26:33.000
The 19 men who accidentally fell into the net called themselves the halfway to hell club, which is pretty badass. The net became this real morale booster. Workers were kind of excited by the notion that people actually cared and the net turned out to be kind of fun, so much so that they had to be ordered not to jump into the net on purpose. There was a major accident during construction where 10 men died and a platform broke free and crushed the bridgemen below. But even with that one-off event, the safety record of the Golden Gate bridge construction was far better than that of the Oakland Bay Bridge where 28 workers died.
那 19 个意外跌入网中的人自称为“半道下地狱俱乐部”（Halfway to Hell Club），这真是酷毙了。这张网真正起到了提振士气的作用。工人们对“居然有人真的在乎他们”这种观念感到有点兴奋，而且这张网结果证明还挺好玩的，以至于他们不得不被勒令禁止故意跳进网里。在施工期间曾发生过一起重大事故，有 10 名工人丧生，当时一个平台断裂，砸中了下方的桥梁工人。但即便有这样一次突发事件，金门大桥施工的安全记录也远比奥克兰海湾大桥（Oakland Bay Bridge，那场事故导致 28 名工人丧生）要好得多。

47
00:26:33.000 --> 00:27:11.000
The Golden Gate Bridge was a real leap forward in valuing the lives of bridgemen and iron workers. And that's what I think of when I see it now. That the greatest human-made structure was made humanely. After the completion of the Golden Gate Bridge, Joseph Strauss returned to his first passion, which was poetry, his most famous poem being The Mighty Task is Done, and it goes, At last the mighty task is done, resplendent in the western sun. The bridge looms mountain high, its tightened piers grip ocean floor, its great steel arms
金门大桥在重视桥梁工人和钢铁工人的生命方面实现了真正的飞跃。这就是现在我看到它时所想到的。人类创造的最伟大建筑，是在充满人性的关怀下建造的。金门大桥完工后，约瑟夫·斯特劳斯回归了他的初心——诗歌，他最著名的诗作是《伟大的任务已经完成》（The Mighty Task is Done），其中写道：“伟大的任务终于完成，在西斜的太阳下熠熠生辉。大桥如山般高耸，紧绷的桥墩紧抓着洋底，钢铁的巨臂

48
00:27:11.000 --> 00:28:08.000
link shore to shore, its towers pierce the sky. It goes on from there. I think he was a better bridge builder than a poet, but I really love that he was both. Strauss died in Los Angeles at the age of 68, one year after the completion of the Golden Gate Bridge. If you ever come here, don't miss it. Walk across it, drive across it, look at it from every angle you can. This bonus episode of 99% of his book was produced by me, Roman Mars. Mixed by Martin Gonzalez. Music by Swan Riau.
连接着彼岸与此岸，高塔刺破了天空。”后面还有很多。我认为他是个比诗人更好的造桥专家，但我真的很喜欢他兼具这两种身份。斯特劳斯在洛杉矶去世，享年 68 岁，正值金门大桥完工一年后。如果你有机会来这里，千万不要错过。走过它，开车过桥，从你能找到的每一个角度去欣赏它。这期《99% Invisible》的特别赞助节目由我——罗曼·马斯制作。马丁·冈萨雷斯（Martin Gonzalez）混音。音乐由天鹅里奥（Swan Riau）创作。

